// the third row of an h 3 rivet spilled into the cell below.
const RIVBIG = RIVETS && process.env.RIVBIG === '1';
// RIVSEAM=1: panel LINES and rivets both -- every step of the seam walk places the boundary line
// and then the panel centre's rivet, so each costs the pass its own projection.
const RIVSEAM = RIVETS && process.env.RIVSEAM === '1';
const RIVCOLS = [[0, 1], [1, 2], [2, 3], [3], [0]];
if (RIVBIG) for (let h = 0; h < 5; h++)
  for (let cv = 0; cv < 5; cv++)
    glyphs.push([...Array(8)].map((_, r) => (h < 4 ? r >= 2 * h && r <= 2 * h + 2 : r === 0) ? seamRow(RIVCOLS[cv]) : seamRow([])));
else if (RIVETS) for (let v = 0; v < 4; v++)
  for (let k = 0; k < 4; k++)
    glyphs.push([...Array(8)].map((_, r) => (r >> 1) === v ? seamRow([k]) : seamRow([])));
// RIVMIN: no rivet on a wall shorter than this many pixels from horizon to top at the rivet's
// column. A fixed-size dot does not shrink with distance, so on a far wall it reads as a blot.
const RIVMIN = +(process.env.RIVMIN ?? (RIVBIG ? 24 : 0));
if (RIVETS && (RIV0 - SEAM0) % 4) throw new Error('RIVETS: RIV0 - SEAM0 must be a multiple of 4');

// ---- the sprite occluders: seven cells of black that CLIP ----
// Bit pair 11, where every wall pixel is 01. In multicolor char mode 00 and 01 count as
// BACKGROUND for sprite priority and 10 and 11 as FOREGROUND, so these clip a sprite whose
// $d01b bit is set while the walls around them do not. Pair 11 takes its colour from the low
// three bits of colour RAM, which is $08 everywhere -- so it draws BLACK, identical to the wall
// it is stamped over. The difference is invisible as colour and exact as occlusion.
//
// Indexed by how many columns from one side are foreground:
//   OCC0+0..3  foreground on the LEFT,  1..4 columns (4 = the whole cell)
//   OCC0+4..6  foreground on the RIGHT, 1..3 columns
// Four boundary positions x two sides would be eight, but "all four from the left" and "all four
// from the right" are the same cell, so it is seven.
//
// Every row is identical, which makes each glyph its own vertical mirror -- so charset B, which
// cpChr builds by reversing each glyph's rows, comes out correct for free.
const OCC0 = glyphs.length;
const occRow = (n, fromRight, bg) => [0, 1, 2, 3].reduce((b, i) => {
  const fg = fromRight ? i >= 4 - n : i < n;
  return b | ((fg ? 3 : bg) << (6 - 2 * i));      // 11 = foreground; bg is the rest of the cell
}, 0);
for (let n = 1; n <= 4; n++) glyphs.push(new Array(8).fill(occRow(n, false, 1)));
for (let n = 1; n <= 3; n++) glyphs.push(new Array(8).fill(occRow(n, true, 1)));
// OCCSYN: synthesise the occluder glyph for a wall-EDGE cell instead of stamping a whole-column
// split over it. The stamp used to quantise to 8-pixel char rows at the wall's base, so a stamp
// at the foot of a wall hung up to 7 px over the floor below it (the user saw 5-6 at X12B Y9CA
// AEF00). The cell at the base already holds a glyph of exactly the right shape -- the wall-edge
// ramp -- with the wrong bit pairs: 01 (wall) where the stamp needs 11 (ink). Copy it into a
// scratch glyph with 01 -> 11 on the columns the stamp covers and store that code. Pixel-exact.
// OCCSYN_N scratch codes, HALF for each screen buffer: the front buffer is on display for the
// whole of the frame being rendered, and a scratch glyph it still shows cannot be rewritten
// under it -- the cell would flip to next frame's outline a refresh early. So buffer A's stamps
// use codes SYN0..SYN0+N/2-1 and buffer B's the rest; a frame needing more than its half falls
// back to the old whole-column stamp for the rest. The user's own idea, costed in August,
// buildable now that the charsets are plain RAM (2026-09-16).
// ---- WHICH CELLS A PRE-BUILT SPLIT CAN ALREADY EXPRESS ----
// The seven occluders above are `new Array(8).fill(occRow(...))` -- every row identical, because
// a column split is a VERTICAL boundary. So a cell glyph whose eight rows are all equal has a
// vertical boundary too, and one of the pre-built splits (black-backed, or the white-backed set
// for a silhouette with floor beyond) covers it exactly. A cell whose rows DIFFER is split across
// rows -- the wall's base -- which no column split can say, and that is the case OCCSYN exists
// for: "the stamp used to quantise to 8-pixel char rows at the wall's base".
// Without this test oSPut sent EVERY ramp cell to synthesis, including plain vertical wall edges,
// which are the commonest stamp there is. Five of them against one edge exhausted the three
// scratch codes a buffer has and the rest went unstamped -- the bottom of an orb showing through
// a wall it was standing behind (the user, 2026-09-30, at X57D Y860 A9C40).
// 1 = a pre-built split says this cell; 0 = only synthesis can.
//
// THE QUESTION IS "DOES THE WALL END INSIDE THIS CELL", not "are the rows identical". The rows
// test was the first version and it is too strict in the way that matters: a sloped VERTICAL
// silhouette edge has rows that differ by a pixel, fails it, and gets synthesised -- and then
// synthesis paints only that cell's wall pixels where the split painted the whole column, which
// is a HOLE punched through a solid occluder (the user, 2026-10-02, at X436 Y65B A1940: "parts
// of the wall that were correct before showing holes now"). A wall-free row is the thing that
// actually distinguishes the two: at a wall's BASE the lower rows have no wall at all, and a
// full-height split paints them anyway -- the overhang. On a sloped edge every row still has
// wall in it, so the split is right to within a pixel.
//
// Paired with osLast, which restricts this to the bottom cell of a stamp: the user's rule is
// "the last occluder in a column AND only if it goes past the bottom edge of the wall", and this
// is the second half of it.
const occWallInRow = row => {           // any of the cell's 4 multicolor pixels at pair 01?
  for (let i = 0; i < 4; i++) if (((row >> (6 - 2 * i)) & 3) === 1) return true;
  return false;
};
const OCC_VRT = glyphs.map(g => g.every(occWallInRow) ? 1 : 0);
if (process.env.OCCVRTDUMP) console.error(
  `  occVrtT: ${OCC_VRT.filter(x => x).length}/${glyphs.length} cells a pre-built split can say ` +
  `(the rows-identical rule gave ${glyphs.filter(g => g.every(r => r === g[0])).length})`);
// OCCSYN: use oSPut -- the per-cell dispatch with the pre-built splits. Always on now: the
// OCCSYN=0 path was the PRE-oSPut code, whose interior loop stamps only cells that are FULLY wall
// ("jsr oSWall / bcc oSCCol") and skips every ramp or step. On a wall edge that is most of them, so
// it stamped nothing at all at the user's pose -- a stale A/B that reads as "occlusion is broken",
// which is the silent trap this file warns about elsewhere. Removed rather than kept as a choice.
// OCCSYNTH=1 brings back the SYNTHESIS ARM: copying a wall-edge cell's own ramp into a scratch
// glyph with 01 -> 11. DEFAULT OFF (the user, 2026-10-01: "I'm inclined to turn it off completely
// and revisit if we ever see anything that needs it again... the occlusion stamp only needs to
// worry about the closer wall line"). With it off, a cell a pre-built split cannot express takes
// the split anyway, which is what OCCFB=block already does when the scratch budget runs out.
// What it was for is the WALL-BASE OVERHANG: at a wall's foot the cell is floor with a sliver of
// wall along its top, and a whole-column split there hangs the stamp up to 7px over the floor
// (reported at X12B Y9CA AEF00). If that does not come back, this can be deleted outright.
// ON by default again 2026-10-02, but ONLY for the bottom cell of a stamp. The user's rule:
// "we shouldn't even be considering synthesis for anything but the last occluder in a column and
// only if it goes past the bottom edge of the wall... take the shape that was already there,
// make a copy, and change its black from background to foreground black. leave the white alone."
//
// The glyph transform was never the problem -- oSPut already does exactly that (01 -> 11 over the
// covered columns, pair 00 untouched). The TRIGGER was: it fired on every cell of every stamp,
// so a mid-wall cell came back with a floor-coloured gap punched through a solid occluder, and
// the entity could draw in the hole. Measured on the user's own test set, where three views that
// render perfectly without it each changed by 48px.
//
// So the gate is osLast -- set only on the final row of the stamp -- plus occVrtT, which says the
// cell has a boundary ACROSS rows at all. A cell that is not the bottom of its column takes the
// pre-built split exactly as it did before.
const OCCSYNTH = process.env.OCCSYNTH !== '0';
// DIAGEARLY, not DIAG: DIAG is declared ~750 lines further down and is BUILT from this one, so
// touching DIAG up here throws "Cannot access 'DIAG' before initialization" -- and only when the
// flag is SET, so the default built fine while OCCSYNTH=1 died inside a level-dump subprocess
// whose stderr the parent discards. The same TDZ trap as the PROFN guard earlier today.
// Registered when it DEVIATES from the default, which is now ON. Left as "if (OCCSYNTH)" after
// the default flipped, every ordinary build flagged itself diagnostic and wrote iray.diag.asm --
// so iray.asm would quietly stop tracking the shipping build, which is the stale-asm trap that
// cost three wrong readings in one session.
if (!OCCSYNTH) DIAGEARLY.push('OCCSYNTH=0');
// EIGHT, four per screen buffer. Six was not a judgement about how many cells an entity needs --
// it was every spare code the charset had, halved. The real figure is up to two per entity (the
// occluding wall's base, and where a distant wall ends inside the same cell), so four per buffer
// covers one wide entity or two small ones. Paid for by refitting the cell codebook at KCELL=197:
// 201 cell codes -> 199, predicted error 6,932,472 -> 6,963,243 px over 512M frames, which is
// 0.014 px/frame either way. The charset's tail is nearly free -- see the char-headroom note.
const OCCSYN_N = +(process.env.OCCSYNN ?? 8);
// OCCSYNEDGE: also synthesise a PARTIALLY COVERED cell, whatever its glyph says.
//
// occVrtT answers "can a column split draw this cell's shape", and that is not the only way a
// pre-built split goes wrong. A split also paints the columns it does NOT cover, with a backing
// chosen black or white -- and that is a guess. Where the uncovered part holds a distant wall's
// end and some floor, black backing flattens both: a black nub sticking out of the wall with
// nothing behind it (the user, 2026-10-02, at X277 Y5BB AF040, "a little black nub sticking out
// to the left under the orb that shouldn't be there").
//
// The user's own framing, which is what this implements: "it's also true if it extends below a
// wall, further in the distance, in that same glyph... it could (should) synthesize a glyph that
// a) leaves white alone and b) only inks fg black where the occluding wall is. It can't just do
// all black." Synthesis has no backing at all -- pixels outside osCov keep the cell's own value,
