// per cast column, so pfTick fires ~20x a frame for each of them and charges its own cost to
// every one -- which is why the fine-grained shares summed to 119% of the frame. Dropping them
// trades the ray breakdown for a top-level split that can be trusted.
const PROFCOARSE = flag('PROFCOARSE');
// SEAMCNT=1: how far down seamSpan each candidate gets. The differences between consecutive
// counters are the reject counts, so no branch restructuring is needed -- each is simply bumped
// at a point only reached by getting past the test above it.
const SEAMCNT = flag('SEAMCNT');
// SEAMDBG=1: one 8-byte record per boundary -- the inputs the projective solve saw and the column
// it produced -- so a jitter can be RECOMPUTED offline at full precision and compared, instead of
// its cause being argued from estimates. Seven records, which covers a frame's boundaries here.
const SEAMDBG = flag('SEAMDBG');
const PROFMAX = flag('PROFMAX');
// ENTPROF=1: split the entities bucket into orbOccAll / orbMove / orbKey / bfsTick / angStep.
// Reuses the bucket indices PROFCOARSE frees, so it must be run WITH PROFCOARSE.
const ENTPROF = flag('ENTPROF');
// A requirement stated in a comment is not a requirement. Run without PROFCOARSE, the fine ray
// buckets stay live inside castRay and write to the SAME indices the entity split uses, so both
// readings are sums of two unrelated phases -- and they look plausible, which is worse.
if (ENTPROF && !PROFCOARSE) throw new Error('ENTPROF=1 needs PROFCOARSE=1: it reuses the bucket ' +
  'indices PROFCOARSE frees, and without it the fine ray buckets are summed into the entity ones');
// OCCLAZY: rebuild the occluder masks only when they can have changed.
// ON by default since 2026-09-03, and it is the single biggest win in the frame. The masks say
// whether an entity is beyond a wall face's PLANE -- no camera term -- so they only change when an
// entity moves or the run tables are re-patched, and on a map that splits nothing neither happens
// most frames. Rebuilding them regardless cost 216 lines a frame on the combined level, 16% of
// everything, because the cost is entities x runs and that level has 4 and 44.
//   orbOccAll 216.0 -> 4.0 lines, whole frame 1315 -> 1052 = 4 video frames = 10 -> 15 fps.
// Verified with its own OCCCHK harness rather than by eye: rebuild anyway, compare byte for byte,
// count differences. 255+ skipped frames checked while walking through cells with both angels
// moving, occBad = 0.
// ENTFOV: reject an entity that is beside the camera before the reciprocal and the column
// arithmetic, from |right| and forward alone. On since 2026-09-15.
// ENTGATE: skip orbMove's frame trig when no entity can possibly be projected.
// ---- ENTCONE: reject an entity on its CELL BEARING, before any multiply at all ----
// orbProj's own rejects are cheap for what they are, but nothing is rejected until forward =
// Ix*cos + Iy*sin and (usually) right = Iy*cos - Ix*sin have been computed: four oTerm/mul8
// products, and CULLCNT says 85% of the entities walked are thrown out after paying for them.
// The entity count IS the candidate count -- orbMove walks every entity every frame, with no
// pre-filter of any kind -- so this is paid per entity per frame, forever.
//
// This is the same question asked from the CELL GRID, where it costs no multiply: the vector
// between two cell CENTRES is exactly the integer delta (both sit at +0.5), so its bearing is a
// pure function of (dx, dy) and lives in a table. Compare that against pa, allow for the fact
// that the player is somewhere in their cell rather than at its centre, and reject outside.
//
// STATELESS ON PURPOSE. Every input is current: no cached value, nothing to dirty, nothing to
// audit across a level load, a death or the fall, and no writer that has to remember to
// invalidate -- which is the bug class orbOccT already shipped once (see orbOccAll). The caching
// version of this test measured no cheaper anyway: "check the key" costs about what "recompute"
// costs. Caching is for the OCCLUSION question, which cannot be answered per frame at all.

// Half the cone, in 256ths of a turn. ENTFOV's bound, NOT the tighter true one: it tests
// |right| > forward * 3/4, so atan(3/4) is the angle it is already verified safe at (FOVCHK, 3/4
// vs the 5/8 first cut that dropped 4 real sprites). Rejecting at the true 116/184 = 0.63 would
// be sound in theory and is not worth being clever about: two extra units of margin on top.
// The bearing of a cell delta, folded to the FIRST QUADRANT: 8x8 at stride 8 = 64 bytes, both
// magnitudes in 0..7. The quadrant comes back from the two signs at runtime for ~12 cycles, which
// is far cheaper than the 1KB a signed table would need. Deltas beyond 7 are HALVED until they
// fit -- the bearing of (dx,dy) is the bearing of (dx/2,dy/2), and the rounding error that
// introduces is folded into the tolerance below.
const CONE_BQ = [...Array(64)].map((_, i) => {
  const dy = i >> 3, dx = i & 7;
  return (dx || dy) ? Math.round(Math.atan2(dy, dx) / (2 * Math.PI) * 256) & 255 : 0;
});
// Tolerance per CHEBYSHEV distance: the cone, plus how far the bearing can be wrong. Two sources,
// both conservative:
//   the player is anywhere in their cell, up to 0.707 from its centre -> asin-ish slack that
//     shrinks with distance. Divided by (c - 0.707), the CLOSEST the entity can be, not c.
//   the halving above rounds each magnitude down by up to 0.5 at the shifted scale, which is
//     0.5 * 2^sh at the real one.
// Indexed by the UNSHIFTED distance so a far entity gets the tight tolerance it deserves; a
// single worst-case number for the whole shift class would have thrown that away.
const CONE_DMAX = 31;                  // covers any delta a 28-wide or 16-tall level can produce
const coneShift = c => c <= 7 ? 0 : c <= 15 ? 1 : 2;
// The slack is MEASURED, not bounded. It was atan(0.707/(c - 0.707)), which measures the angle
// subtended at the CLOSEST possible approach and so double-counts: 20.4 units at Chebyshev 2 where
// the true worst case over the whole cell is 13.1 and even asin(0.707/c) only needs 14.7. Six units
// of 256 sounds small; it was the entire margin by which level 2's orb at (6,8) -- which the user
// could see was plainly off to the right of the viewport -- was being culled at all.
// This is a BUILD-TIME table, so there is no reason to use a bound when the exact figure is a loop:
// for every delta at this Chebyshev distance, the largest bearing change the player can cause by
// standing anywhere in their own cell. Boundary AND interior sampled, because "the maximum of this
// is at a corner" is the kind of assumption that has cost all day.
const coneSlack = c => {
  let m = 0;
  for (let dx = -CONE_DMAX; dx <= CONE_DMAX; dx++) for (let dy = -CONE_DMAX; dy <= CONE_DMAX; dy++) {
    if (Math.max(Math.abs(dx), Math.abs(dy)) !== c) continue;
    const b0 = Math.atan2(dy, dx);
    for (let i = 0; i <= 16; i++) for (let j = 0; j <= 16; j++) {
      const ex = -0.5 + i / 16, ey = -0.5 + j / 16;
      let d = (Math.atan2(dy - ey, dx - ex) - b0) / (2 * Math.PI) * 256;
      d = ((d + 128) % 256 + 256) % 256 - 128;
      m = Math.max(m, Math.abs(d));
    }
  }
  return m;
};
// CONEGATE (now simply how it works): cnTest runs for every entity in entGate rather than in
// orbOne, the verdicts are kept in cnOkT, and the gate SHUTS when nothing is in the cone -- which
// is what skips omTrig. (CONECHK, its in-orbOne checker, no longer built and is gone; GATECHK
// checks the gate's inlined cone against cnTest.)

// BNDCNT=1: per frame, boundaries pinned by the subdivision and how many of them are shared
// corners (both faces at the same height at the boundary: the second end ray is redundant).
const BNDCNT = flag('BNDCNT');
// MARCHCNT=1: marches by SITE -- pivot hint brackets (pivCut) vs bisection midpoints (subMid) --
// plus hints per frame, so the pivot's residue (what bisection still has to find) is measured.
const MARCHCNT = flag('MARCHCNT');
